            0.405,
            0.847,
            0.444
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. If 31z5 is a multiple of 9, where z is a digit, what is the value of z? Explain why there are two answers to this problem."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.45,
            0.387,
            0.468
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ans.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " 3 + 1 + z + 5 = 9 + z"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.202,
            0.471,
            0.334,
            0.489
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "So, z = 0 or 9."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.167,
            0.499,
            0.848,
            0.575
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. “I take a number that leaves a remainder of 8 when divided by 12. I take another number which is 4 short of a multiple of 12. Their sum will always be a multiple of 8”, claims Snehal. Examine his claim and justify your conclusion."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 20,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.581,
            0.652,
            0.6
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ans.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Let the number be a = 12n + 8 and b = 12m – 4"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 21,
          "bbox": [
            0.202,
            0.601,
            0.438,
            0.639
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "So, a + b = 12 (n + m) + 4 = 12 k + 4"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 22,
          "bbox": [
            0.234,
            0.641,
            0.619,
            0.661
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "a + b will not be a multiple of 8 always."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 23,
          "bbox": [
            0.235,
            0.661,
            0.575,
            0.681
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "For example, take k = 2 and check."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 24,
          "bbox": [
            0.167,
            0.69,
            0.847,
            0.748
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3. When is the sum of two multiples of 3, a multiple of 6 and when is it not? Explain the different possible cases, and generalise the pattern."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 25,
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.753,
            0.596,
            0.773
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ans.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Let the two multiples of 3 be 3m and 3n."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 26,
          "bbox": [
            0.199,
            0.779,
            0.847,
            0.82
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "So, 3m + 3n = 3 (m + n). This will be a multiple of 6 only when m + n is a multiple of 2."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 27,
          "bbox": [
            0.167,
            0.829,
            0.847,
            0.867
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4. Sreelatha says, “I have a number that is divisible by 9. If I reverse its digits, it will still be divisible by 9”."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 28,
          "bbox": [
            0.215,
            0.872,
            0.787,
            0.893
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(i) Examine if her conjecture is true for any multiple of 9."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 29,
